    <header>
      <div class="tag">project 07 · anti-cheat research</div>
      <h1>Detecting Hardware Input Emulation</h1>
      <div class="meta"><span>HID security</span><span>anti-cheat</span><span>USB</span><span>behavioural detection</span><span>telemetry</span></div>
    </header>

    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>A whole class of game cheating moves the cheat off the computer entirely. A microcontroller
      such as an Arduino Leonardo, paired with a USB host shield, sits between a real mouse and the
      PC: it reads the player's screen (often via a second device or capture path), decides where the
      crosshair should go, and injects movement while presenting itself to the operating system as an
      ordinary USB mouse. To the host, it <em>is</em> a standard HID. This project is a study of why
      that's so hard to detect, and how anti-cheat development has responded — written from the
      defender's side, because that's the interesting and useful half.</p>

    <h2>Why naïve detection fails</h2>
    <p>The defender's first instinct — "look for the cheat process / injected code" — finds nothing,
      because there's nothing on the machine to find:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>It's a genuine USB device.</strong> The microcontroller enumerates as a real HID, so "is this a legitimate input device?" answers yes at the protocol level.</li>
      <li><strong>No memory contact.</strong> Like the screen-only approach, nothing reads or writes game memory, so kernel anti-cheat's strongest signals have nothing to bind to.</li>
      <li><strong>Input looks "real" by construction.</strong> The movement arrives through the normal HID pipeline, indistinguishable from a mouse at the point of entry.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>So the detection problem can't live at "is there a cheat present?" It has to move to "is this
      input being produced by a human?" and "is this device what it claims to be?"</p>

    <h2>Where detection actually has to live</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Behavioural / movement analysis.</strong> Human aim has signatures — micro-tremor, overshoot-and-correct, reaction-time distributions, acceleration curves that vary shot to shot. Synthetic aim that snaps or tracks too cleanly, or reacts faster than human latency allows, shows up as a statistical outlier even when the input path looks legitimate. This is the most durable signal because it targets the behaviour, not the tool.</li>
      <li><strong>Device &amp; descriptor fingerprinting.</strong> USB descriptors, reporting intervals, vendor/product identifiers and timing characteristics can betray a microcontroller emulating a mouse versus a genuine retail mouse — which is why firmware-level detection has been a focus (more below).</li>
      <li><strong>Server-side modelling.</strong> Moving detection off the trusted-but-bypassable client to aggregate, hard-to-spoof patterns observed across many sessions.</li>
      <li><strong>Trusted-input attestation.</strong> The longer-term direction: raising the bar on proving input genuinely originated from an attested device path rather than a synthesiser.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The Vanguard cat-and-mouse</h2>
    <p>This isn't hypothetical — it's an active front. Public reporting on Riot's Vanguard describes
      these hardware setups masking themselves as a mouse or keyboard and running the cheat off-host,
      and indicates Riot has been targeting Arduino-style devices for years — with detection of these
      devices reported since roughly 2021–2022, thought to work in part through firmware-level
      identification, while cheat developers continue finding circumvention paths. It's a textbook
      detection-engineering arms race: defenders identify a hardware-evasion class, ship detection for
      its observable fingerprints, attackers adjust the fingerprint, and the signal of last resort
      keeps shifting toward behaviour, which is far harder to fake convincingly than a device ID.</p>

    <h2>Proof of concept</h2>
    <p>To ground the analysis in practice rather than only theory, I built and bench-tested an
      end-to-end proof of concept — the microcontroller-and-host-shield input path driven by
      screen-side detection — evaluated strictly against bots in an offline environment. The
      educational value was in the <em>interfacing</em>: bridging a real-time screen-analysis
      pipeline, a serial protocol, and a physical HID peripheral into one working closed loop, and
      handling the coordinate translation and timing between the software and the hardware device.
      That's the transferable skill the project demonstrates — taking a system from idea to working
      hardware/software integration and verifying it empirically, which is how you reason credibly
      about a technique instead of guessing at it.</p>

    <h2>What I took from it</h2>
    <p>The point of the project was to understand, at the hardware/software boundary, why
      "it looks like a real mouse" defeats device-layer checks and forces detection up the stack into
      behaviour and server-side analysis. That reasoning generalises well beyond games:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>HID and peripheral security</strong> — the same trust problem behind malicious-USB / BadUSB defence, where a device's claimed identity can't be taken at face value.</li>
      <li><strong>Bot / automation detection and anti-fraud</strong> — distinguishing human from synthetic interaction is the same core problem, whether the adversary is an aimbot or a credential-stuffing script.</li>
      <li><strong>The trusted-client lesson</strong> — anything the client reports can be spoofed, so high-assurance detection ends up behavioural and server-side. That principle shows up everywhere from anti-cheat to EDR to fraud.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="note"><p><strong>Context &amp; ethics.</strong> A personal project for educational and
      research purposes only, focused on the detection problem — how anti-cheat development identifies
      hardware-emulated input — studied on my own equipment against bots / offline targets only. It was
      not used against live players or distributed. This page documents the defender-side analysis, the
      hardware/software interfacing involved, and the public state of the Vanguard arms race; it is not
      a build guide and no tooling is published here.</p></div>
